# Untitled

Assessing the Effect of a Schwarz P Surface on the Oxygen Electroreduction Performance of Porous Single-Atom Catalysts
The surface curvature of catalysts has a decisive impact on their catalytic performance. However, the influence of a negative-Gaussian-curvature surface on the catalytic performance of porous catalysts has remained unexplored due to the lack of suitable samples. Bicontinuous-structured porous structures can serve as ideal models, but they are known as "Plumber's nightmare" due to their highly difficult preparation. Here, using metal–organic frameworks as the precursor and polymer cubosomes as the template, a bicontinuous mesoporous Fe single-atom catalyst (named bmFeSAC) with a Schwarz P surface is synthesized. The bmFeSAC catalyst has a large specific surface area of \(916\;\mathrm{m}^{2}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) and uniformly distributed \(\mathsf{F e}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{N}_{4}\) active sites with a 1.80 wt.% Fe content. The continuous channels enabled high utilization efficiency of the \(\mathsf{F e}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{N}_{4}\) catalytic sites, while the negative-Gaussian-curvature surface enabled low reaction energy barrier. As an electrocatalyst of the oxygen reduction reaction, bmFeSAC delivered a high half-wave potential of 0.931 V versus. RHE in alkaline electrolyte, reaching the leading level among those of the reported state-of-the-art electrocatalysts. Furthermore, the bmFeSAC-based Zn-air batteries exhibited excellent performance, demonstrating the potential application of bmFeSAC. This study revealed that a bicontinuous-structured porous structure can improve catalytic activity by increasing the utilization ratio of catalytic sites and, more importantly, by regulating the electronic structure of catalyst surfaces through the negative-Gaussian-curvature.
## 1. Introduction
Due to their specific space symmetry, ordered bicontinuous structures have attracted much academic interest since the
Due to their specific space symmetry, ordered bicontinuous structures have attracted much academic interest since the mathematical models of double primitive (DP) and double diamond (DD) structures were built by H. Schwarz in 1865.[1–4] These structures have a single continuous surface separating two intertwined labyrinths that are topologically identical.[5,6] Depending on their different space symmetries, ordered bicontinuous structures have three common categories: the Schwarz primitive (P) surface, the Schwarz diamond (D) surface, and the Schoen gyroid (G) surface.[7–9] When one of the two labyrinths is hollow (i.e., forming an open channel), the resulting structures can be called bicontinuous porous architectures (see an example in Figure 1).[4,10,11] Such structures possess several alluring features: 1) They have 3D continuous frameworks and open channels (similar to the structure of a sponge);[12–15] the former may allow unimpeded electron transfer along the frameworks if they are electrically conductive, while the 3D open channels can enable smooth mass transfer into deep internal areas of functional materials,[16,17] thus increasing the number of accessible functional sites inside the materials.[18–22] 2) Every point on the P, D, or G surface shows negative-Gaussian-curvature.[4] As a typical example, the Schwarz P surface has a saddle shape.[23] These surfaces can result in unique physical properties different from those induced by positive- or
Gaussian-curvature.[4] As a typical example, the Schwarz P surface has a saddle shape.[23] These surfaces can result in unique physical properties different from those induced by positive- or
zero-Gaussian-curvature surfaces.[24–27] These characteristics are desirable for porous catalytic materials that require maximum utilization of their internal active sites and suitable reactive surfaces/interfaces to optimize their catalytic performance.[28] Nevertheless, owing to their complex curve surfaces, bicontinuous architectures have also been called "plumber's nightmare" structures;[29,30] introducing such structures into functional materials has remained challenging regardless of whether top-down (e.g., lithography or photoetching)[1,31] or bottom-up (molecular self-assembly or 3D printing)[32–39] strategies are used, particularly for mesoporous materials with pore sizes in the range of \(2{\approx}50\ \mathrm{nm}\) due to the difficulty in size matching.[37,40–42] To our knowledge, there are few reports on the introduction of bicontinuous porous structures into catalytic materials;[5] in particular, no related studies on single-atom catalysts (SACs) that have high atomic utilization efficiency have been reported. Moreover, previous studies only focused on the effect of conventional curvatures (e.g. positive or zero curvatures) on catalytic performance. The effect of negative-Gaussian-curvature surfaces on the catalytic activity of porous materials has not been explored as yet (Figure S1, Supporting Information). These gaps in knowledge inspire our interest in preparing bicontinuous porous SACs and establishing the unknown structure-activity relationship.[43]
In the present study, we prepared bicontinuous-structured porous carbon-based SACs by using polymer cubosomes (PCs) as the template and metal–organic framework (MOF) as the SAC precursor (Figure 1).[44] The growth of the MOF inside the PC template, followed by carbonization of the MOF@PC composite, produced a new type of mesoporous FeSAC (named bmFeSAC) with an ordered single primitive (SP) structure (i.e., a Schwarz P surface). The bmFeSAC sample had an average pore diameter of \(32\ \mathrm{nm}\), a large specific surface area (SSA) of \(916\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\), and a high Fe content of \(1.80\ \mathrm{wt.}\%\). Using the electrocatalysis of the oxygen reduction reaction (ORR), which is key to fuel cell operation, as an example,[45–52] we studied the impact of the 3D continuous framework/mesochannels and the negative-Gaussian-curvature surface on the catalytic performance of bmFeSAC. Impressively, bmFeSAC exhibited a very high half-wave potential of \(0.931\ \mathrm{V}\) vs. RHE in alkaline electrolyte, higher than those of control materials without a bicontinuous structure as well as those of most reported SACs and commercial \(\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C}\) catalysts. Furthermore, bmFeSAC exhibited excellent catalytic performance in Zn-air batteries, demonstrating its practical application potential. Electrochemical probe experiments demonstrated that the bicontinuous mesoporous structure could allow the smooth transport of reactants and
## 2. Results and Discussion
To understand the effect of the Schwarz P surface (i.e., primitive bicontinuous structure) on the catalytic performance of porous catalysts, we prepared three carbon-based FeSAC samples with different pore structures: \(bmFeSAC\), \(smFeSAC\), and \(mfFeSAC\) (Figure 1); the synthetic procedures are given in the Supporting Information.[44,53,54] The prefixes of the sample names denote the pore structure characteristics of these FeSACs. \(bmFeSAC\) has an ordered SP bicontinuous mesoporous structure; spherical mesopores are introduced into the \(smFeSAC\) sample; and \(mfFeSAC\) is derived from conventional mesopore-free Fe-doped ZIF-8 (ZIF-Fe). For the synthesis of \(bmFeSAC\), the PC templates were prepared by self-assembly of a polystyrene- \(b\)-poly(ethylene oxide) diblock copolymer \((\mathrm{PS}_{153}–b\cdot\mathrm{PEO}_{153}\); the subscript numbers denote the degrees of polymerization) in solution according to previously reported procedures (Figure S2, Supporting Information).[32] Then, the precursors of ZIF-Fe (2-methylimidazole (2-MI), \(\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}\), and \(\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}\) with a molar ratio of 8:1:0.03) were filled in the open channels of PCs and coordinated inside, yielding ZIF-Fe@PC composite spheres.[44] The resultant ZIF-Fe@PC was then subjected to pyrolysis under an Ar atmosphere at \(1000\ \mathrm{~}^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) to remove the PC template and convert the ZIF-Fe phase to carbon skeleton,[55–57] yielding carbon particles that inherited the morphology of the PCs and were anchored with Fe species.[58] Regarding the control samples, the preparation of \(smFeSAC\) was similar to that of \(bmFeSAC\) except for the utilization of spherical micelles formed by \(\mathrm{PS}_{153}–b\cdot\mathrm{PEO}_{114}\) as the mesopore-doping template (Figure S3, Supporting Information).[53] \(mfFeSAC\) was prepared following a conventional protocol without the assistance of any pore-building template.[54]
The formation of the ZIF-Fe frameworks inside the polymer template or the control samples was first demonstrated by X-ray diffraction (XRD) analysis, in which all the diffraction peaks of ZIF-Fe are consistent with those of simulated ZIF-8 (Figure S4, Supporting Information).[59] This result indicates that the PS- \(b\cdot\) - PEO template and the small amount of doped Fe ions used in the formation of ZIF-Fe do not affect the crystal structure of ZIF8.[54] The scanning electron microscopy (SEM) and transmission electron microscopy (TEM) images show that the formed ZIF\(\mathrm{Fe}@\mathrm{PC}\) particles inherited the spherical morphology of PCs, in which the mesochannels were filled with the ZIF phase (Figure S5, Supporting Information); the average particle diameter is \(2.0\pm0.8~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}\) . On the other hand, as the precursors of the con
trol samples, the composite particles of ZIF-Fe and the PS- \(b\cdot\) -PEO spherical micelle (ZIF-Fe@SM) show a dodecahedral morphology with a mean size of \(1.9\pm\:0.5~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}\) , with humps evenly distributed on the surfaces (Figure S6, Supporting Information). The conventional ZIF-Fe particles with a smooth surface display a dodecahedral structure and a similar particle size \((1.9\pm0.9\,\upmu\mathrm{m})\) to those of ZIF-Fe@PC and ZIF-Fe@SM (Figure S7, Supporting Information).
The three ZIF-Fe/composite samples were directly converted to carbon-based FeSACs via pyrolysis under an Ar atmosphere at \(1000\ \mathrm{~}^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\).[60] The microstructures of the different FeSACs were examined via SEM and TEM, as shown in Figure 2 and Figures S8–S10 (Supporting Information). All these FeSACs inherited the particle morphologies of their precursors and exhibit similar average particle sizes \((\approx\!1.5~\upmu\mathrm{m})\) . Specifically, they retained the mesoscopic morphology of their ZIF-Fe precursors, as shown in Figure S11 (Supporting Information). Specifically, \(bmFeSAC\) has a spherical morphology with a mean diameter of \(1.6\pm\:0.7~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}\) (Figure 2a; Figure S8, Supporting Information). The high-magnification SEM image of the surface of \(bmFeSAC\) clearly shows an ordered Schwartz P structure with a uniform pore diameter of \(36\;\mathrm{nm}\) and a wall thickness of \(34\;\mathrm{nm}\) (Figure 2b). Moreover, the high-magnification TEM image of a sliced \(bmFeSAC\) particle confirms the presence of regular tetragonal pore arrays with a unit cell parameter of \(73\;\mathrm{nm}\) (Figure 2c). The small-angle X-ray scattering (SAXS) pattern of \(bmFeSAC\) confirms its SP topology (Schwarz P surface) and gives a unit cell parameter of \(72\,\mathrm{nm}\) (Figure S8d, Supporting Information),[20] which supports the SEM and TEM results. Regarding the control samples, the \(smFeSAC\) particles with an average size of \(1.5\,\pm\,0.4~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}\) show a typical rhombic dodecahedral morphology with spherical mesopores uniformly distributed throughout the whole particle (Figure 2e,f; Figure S9, Supporting Information). The cross-sectional TEM images of \(smFeSAC\) reveal a mean mesopore diameter of \(16~\mathrm{nm}\) (Figure 2g). The SEM and TEM images show that the \(mfFeSAC\) polyhedrons with a mean size of \(1.5\pm\:0.7~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}\) have smooth surfaces without mesopores (Figure 2i,k; Figure S10, Supporting Information). The element mapping images of these FeSACs reveal a homogeneous distribution of Fe in each N-doped carbon skeleton (Figure 2d,h,l).
The \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) absorption/desorption isotherms of \(bmFeSAC\) and \(smFeSAC\) show typical type IV isotherms with a sharp increase of low \(P/P_{0}\) \((<\!0.01)\) and a distinct hysteresis loop (Figure 3a), confirming the presence of a hierarchical micro–mesoporous structure.[61,62] Due to the large mesopore size of \(bmFeSAC\), the adsorption curve of \(bmFeSAC\) increases sharply when \(P/P_{0}\) approaches 1. It can also indicate that \(bmFeSAC\) possesses macropores, which could be attributed to the interspace of the stacked \(bmFeSAC\) particles.[62] \(mfFeSAC\) shows a type I isotherm, reflecting its microporous structure.[63] According to the Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) method, all these FeSAC samples show high SSAs \((\approx\!1000\,\mathrm{m}^{2}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) ; see Table S1, Supporting Information). The pore volumes of \(bmFeSAC\) \((2.26\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1})\) and \(smFeSAC\) \((1.73\,\mathrm{cm}^{3}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1})\) are both higher than that of microporous \(mfFeSAC\) \((0.49\,\mathrm{cm}^{3}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1})\) , indicating an effective improvement in pore volume due to the introduction of mesopores.[64,65] On the basis of the Barrett–Joyner–Halenda (BJH) approach, each FeSAC sample displays a narrow micropore size distribution profile with a peak centered at a diameter of \({\approx}1~\mathrm{nm}\) (Figure 3b). Moreover,
the mesopore size distributions are centered at 32 and \(15\ \mathsf{nm}\) for bmFeSAC and smFeSAC, respectively, which are consistent with the results obtained from the electron microscopy analyses (Figure 2). mfFeSAC does not show any mesopore size distribution peaks (Figure 3b), confirming the absence of mesopores in this sample.
The XRD patterns of all the FeSAC samples show two broad peaks centered at \(\sim\!36^{\circ}\) and \(42^{\circ}\), which are attributed to the (002) and (101) diffraction planes of amorphous carbon, respectively (Figure 3c).[66,67] No signals related to Fe-related particles can be detected for any of the FeSAC samples. The graphitic nature of the carbon skeletons is essentially identical for all the FeSACs, as verified by the Raman spectra (Figure 3d).[68] The XPS survey spectra of all the FeSACs (Figure S12, Supporting Information) display four characteristic peaks at 285, 399, 532, and \(711\ \mathrm{eV}\), which can be assigned to C 1s, N 1s, O 1s, and Fe 2p, respectively.[69] The chemical compositions of these samples are highly similar (Table S2, Supporting Information). The high-resolution N 1s XPS spectra can be fitted to five types of nitrogen species (Figure S13, Supporting Information),[70] and their con
tents in the different samples are similar (Figure 3e). The high-resolution Fe 2p spectra depict two peaks located at 711.0 and \(724.3\ \mathrm{eV}\) (Figure S14, Supporting Information), demonstrating the positive valence states of Fe in these FeSACs.[71] All the FeSACs have similar percentages of Fe species (Figure 3f). These two results indicate that the coordination environments of Fe atoms in the three FeSAC samples are essentially identical. The Fe contents for bmFeSAC, smFeSAC, and mfFeSAC, detected by inductively coupled plasma (ICP)–optical emission spectrometry, are 1.80, 1.78, and \(1.86\ \mathrm{wt.\%}\), respectively (Table S3, Supporting Information).
The states of Fe in the different FeSACs were further identified by aberration-corrected high-angle annular dark-field scanning transmission electron microscopy (ACHAADF-STEM) and X-ray absorption fine spectroscopy (XAFS). Many tiny white spots with a uniform diameter of \(0.2\ \mathrm{nm}\) can be observed in the AC HAADF-STEM images of the FeSACs (bmFeSAC in Figure 4a and the other two samples in Figure S15, Supporting Information). The measured diameter is very close to the theoretical value for an Fe atom, suggesting atomic-level dispersion of Fe atoms.[72,73]
XAFS analysis was employed to investigate the chemical and coordination states of Fe in the different FeSACs. As shown in Figure 4b, the absorption edges of the X-ray absorption near edge structure (XANES) spectra of all the FeSAC samples are located between those of the reference Fe foil and \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\), indicating that the valence state of Fe species is situated between those of metallic Fe and \(\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}\) ions.[74,75] The Fourier transform extended X-ray absorption fine structure (FT-EXAFS) spectra of the FeSAC samples show a main peak at \(1.5\,\mathrm{\AA}\), which can be attributed to Fe−N scattering (Figure 4c).[76–78] Compared with the spectrum of the reference Fe foil, no Fe−Fe peaks at \(2.1\mathrm{~\AA~}\) can be detected for the FeSACs, indicating the absence of Fe−Fe bonds in the FeSAC samples (Figure 4c).[79–83] The FT-EXAFS results confirm the atomic-level dispersion of Fe within the FeSACs, which could be attributed to the fact that Zn ions can serve as a "fence" to expand the distance between adjacent Fe species during pyrolysis, thus preventing their aggregation.[84] Figure 4d shows the experimental and fitted curves of the FT-EXAFS spectra of the different FeSACs, which indicate that the coordination number of Fe is \(\approx4\) (Table S4, Supporting Information). Therefore, the Fe in the FeSACs is considered to be in a state of \(\mathrm{Fe-N}_{4}\) coordination. Notably, Fe atoms tend to aggregate and form nanoparticles during the carbonization of the ZIF-Fe precursors when an excess amount of Fe salts (molar ratio of \(\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}\) to \(\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}>0.03\)) is introduced into ZIF-Fe.
To understand the impact of the Schwarz P surface on the catalytic performance of the porous catalyst, we employed bmFeSAC as an electrocatalyst for ORR, which is a key reaction in fuel cells, and evaluated its catalytic performance.[85–90] First, the ORR catalytic performance was evaluated by rotating disk electrode (RDE)
To understand the impact of the Schwarz P surface on the catalytic performance of the porous catalyst, we employed bmFeSAC as an electrocatalyst for ORR, which is a key reaction in fuel cells, and evaluated its catalytic performance.[85–90] First, the ORR catalytic performance was evaluated by rotating disk electrode (RDE) measurements in \(0.1\mathrm{~M~KOH}\) solution. The linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) curves are presented in Figure 5a. The bmFeSAC catalyst exhibits the highest half-wave potential \((E_{1/2}=0.931\;\mathrm{V})\) and largest limited current density \((J_{\mathrm{k}}=5.57\;\mathrm{mA}\;\mathrm{cm}^{-2})\) among the three FeSAC samples, surpassing those of commercial \(\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C}\) catalysts and most reported SACs (Table S5, Supporting Information). Furthermore, the excellent catalytic activity of bmFeSAC is supported by the much higher kinetic current density \((J_{\mathrm{k}})\) at \(0.85\;\mathrm{V}\) \((27.8\;\mathrm{mA}\;\mathrm{cm}^{-2})\) than those of bmFeSAC \((11.9\;\mathrm{mA}\;\mathrm{cm}^{-2})\), \(m\mathrm{fFeSAC}\) \((3.0\;\mathrm{mA}\;\mathrm{cm}^{-2})\) and \(\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C}\) \((4.4\;\mathrm{mA}\;\mathrm{cm}^{-2})\), indicating more favorable ORR kinetics in the bicontinuous mesoporous catalyst (Figure 5b).[91–93] On the other hand, the rotating ring-disk electrode (RRDE) measurements reveal that bmFeSAC has the highest electron transfer number \((n)\) of 3.95–3.99 and the lowest \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) yield \((<\!3\%)\) at \(0.2–0.8\;\mathrm{V}\) (Figure 5c), which are better than those of \(\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C}\). Accordingly, bmFeSAC follows an ideal \(4\mathrm{e}^{-}\)catalytic pathway for the ORR.[94,95] Moreover, the RDE measurements at various rotating speeds confirm the 4-electron pathway (Figure S16, Supporting Information). The catalytic activity of bmFeSAC exhibits very little decay of \(E_{1/2}\) \((5\;\mathrm{mV})\) after 5,000 catalytic cycles (Figure 5d; Figure S17, Supporting Information). Furthermore, the XPS spectra and TEM images of the bmFeSAC catalyst after 5,000 catalytic cycles prove the retention of its original composition and morphology (Figures S18–S19, Supporting Information), confirming the excellent stability of bmFeSAC. Moreover, the current density of bmFeSAC did not noticeably decrease after \(10\%\) \(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH}\) was added to the electrolyte (Figure S20, Supporting Information). These results demonstrate that bmFeSAC has excellent catalytic stability and methanol tolerance.[96]
Encouraged by the excellent electrocatalytic ORR performance of \(bmFeSAC\) in alkaline electrolyte, the catalytic performance of \(bmFeSAC\) in acidic media was also assessed. The LSV curve of \(bmFeSAC\), recorded in \(0.5\mathrm{~M~H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}\) electrolyte, is presented in Figure S21 (Supporting Information). \(bmFeSAC\) has an \(E_{1/2}\) of \(0.791\mathrm{~V~}\) and a \(J_{\mathrm{L}}\) of \(5.68\mathrm{~mA~cm}^{-2}\), which are superior to those of the two control samples and most reported SACs. Notably, the \(E_{1/2}\) and \(J_{\mathrm{L}}\) values of reported SACs are usually much lower than those \((0.808\,\mathrm{V},5.54\,\mathrm{mA\,cm}^{-2})\) of \(\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C}\) in \(0.5\mathrm{~M~H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}\) electrolyte, whereas the \(E_{1/2}\) and \(J_{\mathrm{L}}\) values of \(bmFeSAC\) are quite close to those of \(\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C}\) (Table S6, Supporting Information). The RRDE measurements demonstrate that the \(bmFeSAC\) catalyst results in an electron transfer number \((n)\) of \(3.92\) and a \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) yield of \(<\!5\%\) at \(0.2–0.8\,\mathrm{V}\) (Figure S22, Supporting Information), comparable to those of \(\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C}\). The desirable \(4\mathrm{e}^{-}\)catalytic pathway of \(bmFeSAC\) was also verified by the Koutechy–Levich equation (Figure S23, Supporting Information). Moreover, \(bmFeSAC\) shows excellent catalytic stability, with only a \(6\;\mathrm{mV}\) decay of \(E_{1/2}\) after \(5\;000\) catalytic cycles (Figure S24, Supporting Information) and methanol tolerance (Figure S25, Supporting Information). The above results indicate that \(bmFeSAC\) is a promising ORR electrocatalyst candidate for both alkaline and acidic electrolytes.[97–99]
The outstanding ORR performance of \(bmFeSAC\) inspires us to seek its practical application potential in energy storage devices, such as Zn-air battery, which is environmentally friendly with high energy density.[100–102] The performance of \(bmFeSAC\) as the air-cathode catalyst was evaluated by assembling the homemade Zn-air batteries (Figure 6a). \(\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C}\) was assessed as the reference catalyst under similar conditions. As shown in Figure 6b, the open circuit potential of the Zn-air batteries with \(bmFeSAC\) and \(\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C}\) as the air-cathode catalysts is \(1.49\) and \(1.44\,\mathrm{~V~}\), respectively. The discharge polarization profiles (Figure 6c) show that the \(bmFeSAC\) based Zn-air battery reaches a peak power of \(218\mathrm{~mW~cm}^{-2}\), much higher than that \((146\mathrm{~mW~cm}^{-2})\) of the \(\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C}\) based one. The specific capacity of the \(bmFeSAC\) based Zn-air battery, normalized by the mass of the consumed Zn foil, is \(808\,\mathrm{mAh\,g^{-1}}\) (corresponding to an energy density of \(1014\,\mathrm{Wh\,kg^{-1}}\)), superior to those \((686\,\mathrm{mAh\,g^{-1}},\,792\,\mathrm{Wh\,kg^{-1}})\) of the control battery (Figure 6d). Moreover, the \(bmFeSAC\) based Zn-air battery only displays a slight change in the voltage gap during 100 cycle tests (Figure 6e). In contrast, the \(\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C}\) based Zn-air battery exhibits an obvious voltage gap. These comparisons indicate that \(bmFeSAC\) can exhibit excellent catalytic performance in Zn-air batteries.
To explain the differences in the catalytic activity of the various FeSACs, several techniques were employed. First, the electrochemical double-layer capacitance \((C_{\mathrm{dl}})\), which is proportional to the electrochemical active surface area (ECSA), was calculated by analyzing the CV curves in the non-Faradaic voltage regions (Figure S26a–c, Supporting Information).[103] The \(C_{\mathrm{dl}}\) of \(bmFeSAC\) \((47.3\,\mathrm{mF\,cm}^{-2})\) is \(2.6\) and \(4.1\) times higher than those of \(smFeSAC\) and \(mfFeSAC\), respectively (Figure S26d, Supporting Information), indicating that the internal ECSA of \(bmFeSAC\) is much more accessible.[19,20,104] The higher ECSA may enable the utilization of more active catalytic sites (Fe single atoms) in the internal space of \(bmFeSAC\). Second, an in situ nitrite stripping method was employed to determine the catalytic site densities (SD) and activities of the different samples (Figure 7).[105–113] This technique depends on the formation of NO–Fe adducts, and
the calculation of SD is based on the charges needed for the reduction of NO,[109,114] which are represented by the shadow areas shown in Figure 7a. The charges required for the reduction of NO over \(bmFeSAC\), \(smFeSAC\), and \(mfFeSAC\), determined by integration of these areas, are \(0.211\), \(0.106\), and \(0.065\,\mathrm{mA\,V\,cm}^{-2}\), respectively. On the basis of Equation S3 (Supporting Information), the corresponding SD values are calculated to be \(1.63\times10^{20}\), \(7.62\times10^{19}\), and \(2.67\times10^{19}\) catalytic sites/gram, respectively. Accordingly, the effective utilization ratios of the catalytic sites can be calculated by dividing SD by Fe wt.% (the total Fe content in FeSAC), which are \(0.81\), \(0.40\), and \(0.14\) for \(bmFeSAC\), \(smFeSAC\), and \(mfFeSAC\), respectively (Figure 7c). Third, the turnover frequency (TOF) value can reflect the intrinsic catalytic activity of a catalyst.[115] The LSV curves of the three catalysts negatively shifted after nitrite poisoning (Figure S27, Supporting Information), resulting in similar changes in the kinetic current densities shown in Figure 7b. The \(bmFeSAC\) catalyst delivers a high ORR activity before poisoning and then undergoes a large activity drop after poisoning, which can be fully recovered by the reductive stripping of NO. The poisoning effect of NO is weaker for \(smFeSAC\) and the weakest for \(mfFeSAC\). TOF is typically defined as the number of electrons transferred per active site per unit time in the electrocatalysis field, which can serve as a descriptor of the intrinsic activity of a catalyst and can be determined via the division, by SD, of the decrease of the ORR kinetic current caused by nitrite poisoning (see Equation S6, Supporting Information).[107] Therefore, the TOF values are independent of SD and thus reflect the intrinsic activity of a single catalytic site. The TOF values of \(bmFeSAC\), \(smFeSAC\), and \(mfFeSAC\) at \(0.80\,\mathrm{V}\) versus. RHE were calculated to be \(0.47\), \(0.31\), and \(0.26\,\mathrm{e\,site}^{-1}\,\mathrm{s}^{-1}\), respectively (Figure 7d). Thus, the 3D continuous open channels clearly play a crucial role in improving SD,[106,108] and, more importantly, in increasing the TOF of the electrochemically accessible Fe catalytic sites. In summary, these results prove that the bicontinuous porous structure significantly increases the utilization ratio and intrinsic activity of the active catalytic sites in the \(bmFeSAC\) catalyst.
High-resolution TEM (HR-TEM) images were obtained to gain deeper insights into the microscopic characteristics of the surfaces of the different FeSACs (Figure 8a–c). The saddle-shaped surface of a bicontinuous structure can be described using nodal equations in terms of the Fourier series, utilizing the structure factor \(F(k)\) with a reciprocal lattice vector \(k\), the phase shift \(\alpha(k)\), and space vector \(r\) (see Equation (1)).[116,117]
\[
\Psi(r)=\sum_{a}F(k)\cos[2\pi k\cdot r-\alpha(k)]=0
\]
where the summation \(\Psi(r)\) is the over set of waves corresponding to the space group of the surface. The Fourier series can be truncated to obtain an approximate mathematical expression for the Schwarz P surface (see Equation (2)).[118]
\[
\cos X+\cos Y+\cos Z=t
\]
where \(X\!=\!2\pi x/a\), \(Y\!=\!2\pi y/a\), \(Z\!=\!2\pi z/a\), \(a\) is the unit cell parameter, and \(t\) is a constant not equal to zero. At each point on a curved surface, the normal curvature \(k\) is defined as the reciprocal of the radius of the osculating circle on the intersecting curve of the
surface and its normal plane, which contains the surface's tangent and the surface's normal. The curvature is positive if the curve turns in the same direction as the surface normal and vice versa. The principal curvatures at the point represented by \(k_{1}\) and \(k_{2}\) are the maximum and minimum values of the normal curvature. The Gaussian curvature \(K\) at a given point, an intrinsic measure of curvature, is the product of the principal curvatures, as shown in Equation (3).
\[
K=k_{1}\cdot k_{2}
\]
For hyperbolic (saddle-shaped) surfaces, the principal curvatures \(k_{1}\) show opposite signs, indicating that the Gaussian-curvature is \(K\,<\,0\). Therefore, a hyperbolic surface is dis
tinguished from a spherical surface by its positive-Gaussian-curvature as well as a flat one by its zero-Gaussian-curvature. Considering the intrinsic hyperboloid characteristics of a Schwarz P (saddle-shaped) surface (Figure 2b), we focused on the observation of the curved surfaces at the nodes of the carbon frameworks in bmFeSAC via HR-TEM. As shown in Figure 8a, at microscopic scale, some nearly hyperboloidal carbon lattice stripes with negative-Gaussian-curvature can be observed on the Schwarz P surface of bmFeSAC, which are different from the dense microporous structure of its ZIF precursor (Figure S28, Supporting Information). For smFeSAC, concentric circular lattice stripes can be observed in the HR-TEM image in Figure 8b, while planar parallel carbon lattices on the particle surfaces of mFeSAC are visible in the HR-TEM images (Figure 8c).
Accordingly, three different surface models containing \(\mathrm{FeN}_{4}\) catalytic sites are proposed in Figure 8d–f; the models have negative \((n\mathrm{FeN}_{4})\), positive \((p\mathrm{FeN}_{4})\), and zero \((z\mathrm{FeN}_{4})\) Gaussian-curvatures, respectively. As illustrated in Figure 8d, each point on the hyperboloid surface has two different principal curvatures with opposite directions and a mean curvature of zero \((k_{1}=-k_{2})\), leading to \(K<0\), which determines the negative-Gaussian-curvature. For smFeSAC, the principal curvatures of each point on the surface of a spherical mesopore are essentially identical \((k_{1}=k_{2}\neq0)\), giving \(K>0\), which denotes a positive-Gaussian-curvature (Figure 8e). For mFeSAC, both \(k_{1}\) and \(k_{2}\) on every surface point are equal to zero; thus, \(K=0\) indicates zero-Gaussian-curvature of the planar carbon surfaces (Figure 8f).
negative-Gaussian-curvature surface can reduce the adsorption energies of \(\mathrm{FeN}_{4}{\cdot}\mathrm{OH}^{*}\) intermediates \(\mathrm{^{*}O H}\) is absorbed on the \(\mathrm{FeN}_{4}\) site) to yield a more favorable ORR process.
## 3. Conclusion
In summary, we prepared a bicontinuous-structured mesoporous Fe SAC (bmFeSAC) with a Schwarz P surface. The bmFeSAC catalyst had a highly ordered porous structure with a large specific surface area \((916~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1})\) and a dense distribution of Fe single atoms \((1.80\,\mathrm{wt.}\%)\) . The utilization percentage of the catalytic sites in bmFeSAC reached as high as \(81\%\) , which was \({\approx}6\) times higher than that of the control mesopore-free catalyst, confirming the advantageous effect of the bicontinuous mesoporous structure on mass transfer. Furthermore, the negative-Gaussian-curvature surface regulated the electronic structure and reduced the reaction energy barrier, thus improving the intrinsic catalytic activity of bmFeSAC to ca. 2 times higher than those of the control catalysts. These advantages endowed bmFeSAC with high catalytic performance for ORR, with a top-level half-wave potential of \(0.931\,\mathrm{V}\) vs. RHE, 4-electron pathway selectivity, and long cycling stability with negligible decay after 5000 cycles in alkaline electrolyte. Our study highlights the key roles of negative-Gaussian-curvature surfaces in improving catalytic performance and provides new guidances for the preparation of high-performance catalysts by introducing 3D continuous channels with negative-Gaussian-curvature surfaces.
